In the quaint town of Rainshadow, Sundays were unlike any other day of the week. The residents had long ago adapted to the peculiar weather pattern that governed their lives: rain fell exclusively on Sundays. This was not just a light drizzle or a gentle shower; it was a downpour that could last for hours, turning the streets into rivers and the parks into vast, soggy fields.

The townsfolk had developed various strategies to cope with the weekly deluge. On Saturday nights, they would hang their washing indoors, secure their outdoor furniture, and ensure that their umbrellas were within easy reach. By Sunday morning, everyone was ready with their waterproof gear, and the local shops did a brisk business in raincoats, boots, and slickers.

